--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PARITY (reproduced from the web source — cross-platform VALUE parity, not "corrected")
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Live now : unixSeconds = Math.floor(now/1000) ; iso = now.toISOString() (UTC, .SSS'Z') ; ticks every 1s.
  fromUnix : if(!unix) null ; ms = unix.length>10 ? parseInt(unix,10) : parseInt(unix,10)*1000 ;
             new Date(ms) (Invalid → null). Rows: Iso = toISOString ; Local = formatDateTime ; Relative.
  fromIso  : new Date(iso) (Invalid → null). Rows: Unix = Math.floor(getTime()/1000) ; Local ; Relative.
  getRelativeTime: diff=|now-d| ; s=floor(diff/1000) <60→"Xs ago" ; m<60→"Xm ago" ; h<24→"Xh ago" ; else "Xd ago".
  Now btn  : unix = String(Math.floor(Date.now()/1000)) ; iso = new Date().toISOString().

  i18n keys extracted from the web source (table "TimestampTool"): "Timestamp", "Timestamp Desc", "Now",
  "Unix Timestamp", "Iso", "Local", "Relative", "Iso Timestamp", "Unix". The web en.json carries
  Timestamp/Now/Iso/Local/Relative/Unix verbatim and has NO entry for "Timestamp Desc"/"Unix Timestamp"/
  "Iso Timestamp", so i18next renders those keys as-is — the native fallbacks reproduce that exactly (parity).
  Native-only chrome (still localized via the facade, web English as default): the 4 empty/invalid field hints,
  the 4 relative-unit formats (the web hardcodes "Xs ago" in getRelativeTime; native keeps the identical default
  but stays translatable), and the VoiceOver summaries. The view holds no bare English literals.

  States: this is a pure synchronous compute tool — the web source has NO data fetching (no useQuery), so
  loading / remote-error / stale / offline are N/A by parity (no async source exists to be in those states).
  The web source's per-field branch ({fromUnix && …} / {fromIso && …}) is reproduced and tested as three
  always-rendered states per field: content (parsed) / empty (no input → guidance hint) / invalid (non-empty
  unparseable → a friendly "couldn't read that" hint). Native never shows a blank box.

